      "text": "To find the vector on the line at a different \\( t \\) (in this case, \\( t = -7 \\)), we need to determine the equation of the line and its parameters. The points on the line at different values of \\( t \\) provide us with essential information.\n\nGiven that the vector on the line at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} \\) and at \\( t = 3 \\) is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} \\), we can first find the difference in the vectors for \\( t = 3 \\) to \\( t = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis difference vector represents the change in the vector as \\( t \\) increases from 2 to 3. Therefore, we can express the difference as \\( \\Delta t = 1 \\) and the change in the vector as \\( \\Delta (t, v) = (2, -8) \\). The relationship between \\( t \\) and the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}\\) can be written as:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} x_2 \\\\ y_2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{t - t_2}{2 - 3}\n\\]\nwhich gives:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 2 \\\\ 4 - (-8) \\end{pmatrix} \\frac{t - 2}{3 - 2} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 12 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 12 \\end{pmatrix} (t - 2)\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\( t = -7 \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 12 \\end{pmatrix} (-7 - 2) = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 12 \\end{pmatrix} (-9) = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -9 \\\\ -108 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 - 9 \\\\ -8 - 108 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ -116 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ -116 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
