      "text": "To solve for the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\), we need to determine the parametric equations that describe the line passing through the points at \\( t = 2 \\) and \\( t = 3 \\). \n\nGiven:\n- At \\( t = 2 \\), the vector is:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n  \\]\n- At \\( t = 3 \\), the vector is:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\n  \\]\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) of the line by subtracting the vector at \\( t = 2 \\) from the vector at \\( t = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equations for the line can be written as:\n\\[\n\\vec{r}(t) = \\begin{pmatrix} x_0 \\\\ y_0 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\mathbf{d}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x_0 \\\\ y_0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is the point on the line at \\( t = 2 \\).\n\nNext, we determine the constant \\(\\mathbf{d}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\( t = -7 \\) into the parametric equation to find the vector at \\( t = -7 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\vec{r}(-7) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (-7) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\( (-7) \\) times the direction vector:\n\\[\n-7 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -14 \\\\ 56 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nAdd this result to the vector at \\( t = 2 \\):\n\\[\n\\vec{r}(-7) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -14 \\\\ 56 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 14 \\\\ 4 + 56 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
