      "text": "To find the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\), we first need to determine the direction vector of the line. The direction vector is given by the change in the vector components as \\( t \\) changes from 2 to 3. The vector at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} \\) and the vector at \\( t = 3 \\) is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} \\). The change in the \\( x \\)-coordinate from \\( t = 2 \\) to \\( t = 3 \\) is \\( 3 - 1 = 2 \\), and the change in the \\( y \\)-coordinate is \\( -4 - 4 = -8 \\). So, the direction vector is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the vector at \\( t = -7 \\). The direction vector changes linearly with \\( t \\), so if we know the direction from \\( t = 2 \\) to \\( t = -7 \\), we can use it to find the vector. The change in \\( t \\) from 2 to -7 is \\( -7 - 2 = -9 \\). Therefore, the direction vector from \\( t = 2 \\) to \\( t = -7 \\) is \\( -9 \\) times the direction vector from \\( t = 2 \\) to \\( t = 3 \\). This means the direction vector at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\\[\n-9 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ 72 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow, we sum this direction vector with the vector at \\( t = 2 \\) to get the vector at \\( t = -7 \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ 72 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 18 \\\\ 4 + 72 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -17 \\\\ 76 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThus, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -17 \\\\ 76 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]",
